00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:31.297
<i> ♪ Wade in the water ♪</i>

00:00:31.330 --> 00:00:33.599
<i> ♪ Wade in the water children ♪</i>

00:00:33.632 --> 00:00:35.835
<i> ♪ Wade in the water ♪</i>

00:00:35.868 --> 00:00:38.405 align:start size:81% position:19%
<i> ♪ God's gonna
 trouble the water ♪</i>

00:00:39.071 --> 00:00:40.873 line:0%
<i> ♪ Oh, oh, oh, oh ♪ ♪</i>

00:00:40.906 --> 00:00:44.744 line:0%
DOUGLASS: Under his wise
and beneficent rule we saw

00:00:44.777 --> 00:00:49.315 line:0%
ourselves gradually lifted
from the depths of slavery to

00:00:49.348 --> 00:00:52.385 align:start size:84% position:16%
the heights of
liberty and manhood...

00:00:53.185 --> 00:00:58.058
Though we waited long,
we saw all this and more.

00:01:06.899 --> 00:01:10.870 align:start position:0%
ROBERTS: Abraham Lincoln is
known as "the great emancipator"

00:01:10.903 --> 00:01:13.640 align:start size:88% position:13%
but the moniker
wasn't earned overnight.

00:01:14.307 --> 00:01:17.877
It would take the tireless work
of the abolitionist movement,

00:01:17.910 --> 00:01:21.881 align:start size:97% position:3%
led by Frederick Douglass,
who himself was born enslaved,

00:01:22.348 --> 00:01:25.785 align:start size:97% position:3%
to guide Lincoln
towards the cause of freedom.

00:01:26.051 --> 00:01:28.955 line:0%
DOUGLASS: I am alike familiar
with the whip and chain of

00:01:28.988 --> 00:01:34.494 line:0%
slavery and the lash and sting
of public neglect and scorn;

00:01:34.527 --> 00:01:36.996 align:start size:81% position:19%
that my back is
marked with the one,

00:01:37.029 --> 00:01:40.200 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
and my soul is
fettered with the other.

00:01:40.466 --> 00:01:42.368 align:start size:81% position:19% line:0%
ROBERTS: After
years of opposition,

00:01:42.401 --> 00:01:45.104 align:start size:88% position:13%
these two giants
would find common ground

00:01:45.137 --> 00:01:47.240 align:start size:78% position:22%
and change the
course of history.

00:01:48.474 --> 00:01:51.644 align:start size:91% position:9%
Ten years before the
Emancipation Proclamation
was enacted...

00:01:52.511 --> 00:01:54.914 line:0%
LINCOLN: If all earthly
power were given me,

00:01:54.947 --> 00:01:56.749 line:0%
I should not know what to do,

00:01:56.782 --> 00:01:59.118 line:0%
as to the existing institution.

00:01:59.151 --> 00:02:01.888 line:0%
DOUGLASS: O! had I the ability,

00:02:01.921 --> 00:02:04.724 line:0%
and could I reach
the nation's ear,

00:02:04.757 --> 00:02:09.395 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
I would today pour out a fiery
stream of biting ridicule,

00:02:10.329 --> 00:02:15.401 align:start size:94% position:6%
blasting reproach, withering
sarcasm and stern rebuke.

00:02:15.434 --> 00:02:19.072 align:start size:88% position:13%
For it is not light that
is needed, but fire;

00:02:19.872 --> 00:02:23.209 align:start size:94% position:6%
it is not the gentle shower,
but thunder.

00:02:23.576 --> 00:02:25.645
(thunder)

00:02:35.588 --> 00:02:37.190
MORRIS Jr.: Frederick Douglass
never had a pair of pants or

00:02:37.223 --> 00:02:39.926
shoes until he was about
six or seven years old.

00:02:39.959 --> 00:02:43.129
He would sleep headfirst in
an old corn sack with his

00:02:43.162 --> 00:02:45.631
feet hanging out on
cold winter nights

00:02:45.664 --> 00:02:47.833
to try and keep himself warm.

00:02:47.866 --> 00:02:50.903 align:start size:97% position:3%
ROBERTS: Teaching the
enslaved to read was illegal.

00:02:50.936 --> 00:02:54.674 align:start size:97% position:3%
So young Frederick secretly
traded what little food he had

00:02:54.707 --> 00:02:57.143
in exchange for
reading lessons.

00:02:58.344 --> 00:03:00.546
MORRIS Jr.: It just shows
how much he understood

00:03:00.579 --> 00:03:03.449
how education was going
to be his key to freedom.

00:03:03.749 --> 00:03:06.586
ROBERTS: And a key
to freedom it was...

00:03:06.819 --> 00:03:09.789 align:start size:91% position:9%
At the age of 20, Douglass
made a daring escape

00:03:09.822 --> 00:03:11.991 align:start size:91% position:9%
and went north,
settling in Massachusetts.

00:03:13.058 --> 00:03:16.062
There, he soared: From orator...

00:03:16.095 --> 00:03:19.131 align:start size:88% position:13%
DOUGLASS: The one cannot
be truly free while

00:03:19.164 --> 00:03:20.900
the other is a slave.

00:03:21.333 --> 00:03:23.102 align:start size:81% position:19%
ROBERTS: To
newspaper editor...

00:03:23.302 --> 00:03:26.306
DOUGLASS: Truth
is of no color.

00:03:26.506 --> 00:03:28.140 align:start size:84% position:16%
ROBERTS: And
best-selling author...

00:03:28.173 --> 00:03:30.776
DOUGLASS: From that moment,
I understood the pathway

00:03:30.809 --> 00:03:33.146
from slavery to freedom.

00:03:33.346 --> 00:03:36.749 align:start size:84% position:16%
ROBERTS: He was the
central voice of the
abolitionist movement.

00:03:37.015 --> 00:03:39.018 align:start position:0%
MORRIS Jr.: He had this natural
gift for communication.

00:03:39.051 --> 00:03:41.287
He was eloquent.
He was charismatic.

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:43.823 align:start size:97% position:3%
STAUFFER: There wasn't a venue
building large enough to

00:03:43.856 --> 00:03:46.659 align:start size:81% position:19%
hold people who
wanted to hear him.

00:03:48.027 --> 00:03:50.129 align:start size:88% position:13%
DOUGLASS: Would you have
me argue that man is

00:03:50.162 --> 00:03:52.265
entitled to liberty?

00:03:52.698 --> 00:03:56.669
That he is the rightful
owner of his own body?

00:03:57.236 --> 00:04:00.707 align:start size:88% position:13%
Must I argue the
wrongfulness of slavery?

00:04:01.574 --> 00:04:05.111 align:start size:81% position:19%
At a time like this,
scorching irony,

00:04:05.144 --> 00:04:08.581 align:start size:81% position:19%
not convincing
argument is needed.

00:04:09.081 --> 00:04:10.683
(applause).

00:04:12.318 --> 00:04:15.221
ROBERTS: Much of Douglass'
irony was directed towards

00:04:15.254 --> 00:04:18.124
Washington, DC and
Abraham Lincoln,

00:04:18.691 --> 00:04:21.227 size:78% position:39%
the anti-slavery
president who took office

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:24.664 align:start size:84% position:16%
just as the Civil War
began in 1861.

00:04:25.297 --> 00:04:27.166 align:start size:91% position:9%
STAUFFER: Anti-slavery is
very different than

00:04:27.199 --> 00:04:28.801
being an abolitionist.

00:04:28.834 --> 00:04:32.872 align:start size:94% position:6%
it was very gradual that
most anti-slavery candidates

00:04:32.905 --> 00:04:35.775 align:start size:84% position:16%
believed in the
abolition of slavery.

00:04:36.675 --> 00:04:38.277
LINCOLN: I do not
suppose that...

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:40.913 align:start size:94% position:6%
ultimate extinction would
occur in less than 100 years

00:04:40.946 --> 00:04:42.381
at the least.

00:04:42.414 --> 00:04:44.817 align:start position:0%
STAUFFER: That would have
put the end of slavery in 1958.

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:49.188 align:start size:94% position:6%
And for Frederick Douglass,
that's outrageous.

00:04:49.221 --> 00:04:52.658 align:start size:97% position:3%
DOUGLASS: Mr. Lincoln is quite
a genuine representative of

00:04:52.691 --> 00:04:54.593
American prejudice...

00:04:54.626 --> 00:04:58.765
and far more concerned for
the preservation of slavery.

00:05:00.132 --> 00:05:03.536 align:start position:0%
ROBERTS: Douglass was relentless
in his fight for freedom...

00:05:03.569 --> 00:05:06.539
and with the steady drumbeat
of the abolitionist movement

00:05:06.572 --> 00:05:10.209 align:start size:97% position:3%
in his ear, Lincoln signed
the Emancipation Proclamation,

00:05:11.243 --> 00:05:14.547
freeing enslaved people
in the southern states and

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:18.250
for the first time, making
it legal for all black men

00:05:18.283 --> 00:05:21.120
to fight in the Civil War.

00:05:21.153 --> 00:05:24.724 align:start size:94% position:6%
These new soldiers injected
the Union army with

00:05:24.757 --> 00:05:26.926
much needed manpower.

00:05:26.959 --> 00:05:30.797 align:start size:97% position:3%
But still, they faced
discrimination and disparity.

00:05:31.164 --> 00:05:34.266 align:start size:88% position:13%
STAUFFER: Douglass
is so outraged that
he makes a trip to D.C.

00:05:34.299 --> 00:05:37.403 align:start size:91% position:9%
and decides he wants to
meet with Lincoln himself.

00:05:41.507 --> 00:05:43.042
ROBERTS: To his surprise,

00:05:43.075 --> 00:05:45.678 align:start size:81% position:19%
Lincoln agreed to
see him immediately.

00:05:45.711 --> 00:05:47.546
MORRIS Jr.: He was ushered up
past all of the people that

00:05:47.579 --> 00:05:50.182
were waiting to see
Lincoln that day.

00:05:50.215 --> 00:05:53.018 align:start size:97% position:3%
And just imagine this
visual of this Black man just

00:05:53.051 --> 00:05:54.587
bounding up the steps,

00:05:54.620 --> 00:05:56.355
and people are looking
at him like who is that?

00:05:56.388 --> 00:05:58.991 align:start size:88% position:13%
STAUFFER: Lincoln
begins the conversation.

00:05:59.024 --> 00:06:01.060 align:start size:84% position:16%
And he says,
"Hello, Mr. Douglass,"
essentially,

00:06:01.093 --> 00:06:04.864
"It's good finally to meet you.
I've read your writings a lot.

00:06:04.897 --> 00:06:07.800
I know you've been
very critical of me."

00:06:08.167 --> 00:06:11.471
ROBERTS: Their one-on-one left a
lasting impression on both men,

00:06:12.271 --> 00:06:16.209
and Lincoln vowed to improve the
conditions for black soldiers.

00:06:17.843 --> 00:06:19.945 align:start size:94% position:6%
DOUGLASS: My whole interview
with the president was

00:06:19.978 --> 00:06:22.715 align:start size:88% position:13%
gratifying and did much
to assure me that

00:06:22.748 --> 00:06:27.119 align:start size:94% position:6%
slavery would not survive
the war and that the country

00:06:27.152 --> 00:06:30.690
would survive both
slavery and war.

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:35.428 align:start size:88% position:13%
ROBERTS: A year later,
it is Lincoln who needs
Douglass's help.

00:06:36.061 --> 00:06:37.930
STAUFFER: He feels he is
going to lose reelection.

00:06:37.963 --> 00:06:39.298
and essentially
says you know,

00:06:39.331 --> 00:06:42.768 align:start size:88% position:13%
"I need you, I think the
war is going badly."

00:06:42.801 --> 00:06:45.471
ROBERTS: Lincoln entrusted
Douglass with creating a plan

00:06:45.504 --> 00:06:47.706 align:start size:88% position:13%
for aiding the escape of
enslaved people from

00:06:47.739 --> 00:06:52.011
rebel territory, so that they
could join the Union cause.

00:06:52.511 --> 00:06:57.650
STAUFFER: Douglass writes a
detailed letter to Lincoln

00:06:57.683 --> 00:07:02.422
spelling out his vision
of these Special Forces.

00:07:04.122 --> 00:07:06.692
DOUGLASS: He did not let me
feel for a moment that there

00:07:06.725 --> 00:07:09.762
was any difference in
the color of our skins!

00:07:10.429 --> 00:07:13.733 line:0%
The President is a
most remarkable man.

00:07:15.267 --> 00:07:17.770 align:start position:0%
ROBERTS: With Douglass' support,
Lincoln would go on to

00:07:17.803 --> 00:07:20.940 align:start size:88% position:13%
turn the tide of the war
and win re-election.

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:23.275
The following year,

00:07:23.308 --> 00:07:25.377
Douglass returned to
Washington once more...

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:27.780
to attend the inauguration.

00:07:28.247 --> 00:07:31.050 align:start position:0%
DOUGLASS: Now in this
multitude of elite of the land,

00:07:31.083 --> 00:07:35.121
I felt myself a
man among men.

00:07:35.387 --> 00:07:40.226
STAUFFER: It's the first time a
President devotes most of the

00:07:40.259 --> 00:07:42.929
speech to African Americans.

00:07:43.429 --> 00:07:47.867
LINCOLN: One eighth of
the whole population
were colored slaves...

00:07:48.234 --> 00:07:51.136 align:start size:94% position:6%
KENNETH: Frederick Douglass
heard his words and

00:07:51.169 --> 00:07:53.940 align:start size:78% position:22%
his language in
Lincoln's address.

00:07:54.774 --> 00:07:58.477 align:start size:94% position:6%
LINCOLN: Until every drop
of blood drawn with the lash

00:07:58.510 --> 00:08:02.381
shall be paid by another
drawn with the sword...

00:08:03.014 --> 00:08:04.717
MORRIS Jr.: The work that he
had done over so many years.

00:08:04.750 --> 00:08:07.486
He was now hearing this at a
very important moment in time

00:08:07.519 --> 00:08:08.821
in the nation's history.

00:08:08.854 --> 00:08:13.192
STAUFFER: Douglass is invited to
the White House for a reception.

00:08:13.225 --> 00:08:16.028 align:start size:84% position:16%
He sees Douglass enter
and he yells out...

00:08:16.061 --> 00:08:19.164 align:start size:97% position:3%
LINCOLN: Here comes my friend,
Frederick Douglass...

00:08:19.197 --> 00:08:22.134 align:start size:97% position:3%
There is no man in the
country whose opinion I value

00:08:22.167 --> 00:08:24.270
more than yours.

00:08:24.703 --> 00:08:27.907
DOUGLASS: Mr. Lincoln,
that was a sacred effort.

00:08:32.344 --> 00:08:36.082
<i> ♪ Oh, freedom ♪</i>

00:08:37.182 --> 00:08:41.854
<i> ♪ Oh, freedom ♪</i>

00:08:42.454 --> 00:08:46.626
<i> ♪ Oh, freedom ♪ ♪</i>

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:50.896 align:start size:97% position:3%
MORRIS Jr.: Frederick Douglass
spoke truth to power,

00:08:50.929 --> 00:08:53.532 align:start size:88% position:13%
and he never stopped
fighting for his people,

00:08:53.565 --> 00:08:55.801
and he understood that
the struggle continued.

00:08:55.834 --> 00:08:56.935
He said...

00:08:56.969 --> 00:09:00.339 align:start size:94% position:6%
DOUGLASS: Without struggle,
there is no progress...

00:09:00.372 --> 00:09:03.409 align:start size:84% position:16%
Power concedes nothing
without a demand.

00:09:04.209 --> 00:09:07.413
It never did and
it never will.

